<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Charles Darwin</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1809">1809</a>–<a href="/years/1882">1882</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Charles Darwin was an English naturalist whose theory of evolution by natural selection transformed biology. As naturalist on the survey voyage of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hms-beagle">HMS Beagle</a> from 1831 to 1836, he visited the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galapagos-islands">Galápagos Islands</a> in 1835, where the variation of finches, mockingbirds and tortoises between islands later shaped his ideas. He set them out in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/on-the-origin-of-species">On the Origin of Species</a> in 1859.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/charles-darwin.jpg" alt="Charles Darwin" width="320" height="421" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Henry Maull (1829–1914) and John Fox (1832–1907) (Maull &amp; Fox) [3]; derivative work by Beao · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Charles_Darwin_seated_crop.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Galápagos Islands, visited by <b>Charles Darwin</b> aboard HMS Beagle in 1835, supplied observations that later shaped his theory of evolution by natural selection, and in 1978 the islands and the historic centre of Quito were among the first sites inscribed on UNESCO’s World Heritage List.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-ecuador">Republic of Ecuador · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Science and thought flourished: <b>Charles Darwin</b>’s On the Origin of Species (1859), the work of Michael Faraday and James Clerk Maxwell on electromagnetism, and the economics of David Ricardo and John Stuart Mill.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-kingdom-of-great-britain-and-ireland">United Kingdom of Great Britain and Ireland · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Charles Darwin</b>’s On the Origin of Species (1859) undermined religious certainty and the idea of human uniqueness, and Karl Marx’s Das Kapital (1867) argued that capitalism was full of contradictions.</blockquote><a href="/movement/modernism">Modernism · Background</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Charles Darwin</b> published evolution by natural selection in 1859, and the tree records Alfred Russel Wallace's work in the Maluku Islands in 1858.</blockquote><a href="/essays/who-invented-it-first">Who Invented It First?</a></li><li><blockquote>HMS Beagle left Plymouth on a surveying voyage with the 22-year-old naturalist <b>Charles Darwin</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-27">27 December in history</a></li><li><blockquote>John Murray published <b>Charles Darwin</b>'s On the Origin of Species, setting out evolution by natural selection.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-24">24 November in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in Down House, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.33,0.05&amp;year=1856" aria-label="Down House on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4551 386 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Charles Darwin"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5001 656h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5001 656h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=51.33,0.05&amp;year=1856">What was here in 1856? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…from so simple a beginning endless forms most beautiful and most wonderful have been, and are being, evolved.&#8221;</p><footer>On the Origin of Species (1859), closing words &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/1228/pg1228-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…ignorance more frequently begets confidence than does knowledge: it is those who know little, and not those who know much, who so positively assert that this or that problem will never be solved by science.&#8221;</p><footer>The Descent of Man (1871), introduction &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/2300/pg2300-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Man still bears in his bodily frame the indelible stamp of his lowly origin.&#8221;</p><footer>The Descent of Man (1871), closing words &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/2300/pg2300-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>3 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…if the misery of our poor be caused not by the laws of nature, but by our institutions, great is our sin…&#8221;</p><footer>The Voyage of the Beagle (1845), ch. 21, on slavery in Brazil &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/944/pg944-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Those who look tenderly at the slave owner, and with a cold heart at the slave, never seem to put themselves into the position of the latter; what a cheerless prospect, with not even a hope of change!&#8221;</p><footer>The Voyage of the Beagle (1845), ch. 21, on slavery in Brazil &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/944/pg944-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Nevertheless the difference in mind between man and the higher animals, great as it is, certainly is one of degree and not of kind.&#8221;</p><footer>The Descent of Man (1871), part 1, ch. 4 &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/2300/pg2300-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 535 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
